  <h2 id="galaxy-revision">Galaxy's 1 August revision covers a wider set than this page</h2>

  <p>
    At 18:38 UTC on 1 August 2026,
    <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-third-wave-revision/">Galaxy Research reported a third sweep wave</a>
    of {fig('thirdWaveBtc')} and revised its estimated observed size of the
    incident to {fig('galaxyRevisedTotalBtc')}, stated as approximately
    US$88.6m, across {fig('galaxyRevisedAddresses')}.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Galaxy's third wave, its revised total and address count, and the method limit it states for them"
      source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August thread; the revised totals are Galaxy's attributed estimate and are not reconciled transaction by transaction here"
      href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-third-wave-revision/"
    />
    That supersedes the
    1,082.65 BTC total Galaxy published on 31 July, which is the figure the
    accounting above reconciles. The two are different scopes rather than
    alternative measurements of one set: Galaxy's spans three waves, including a
    third wave that is not in the pinned dataset here, so the totals should not
    be added or read against each other.
  </p>

  <p>
    Galaxy states its own method limit in the same thread. It bounds every
    Galaxy figure quoted on this page and is the first thing to read beside the
    revised total, not a footnote to it.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    The data "is derived solely from analyzing Bitcoin block data and the
    unspent-output set". Galaxy has "not utilized compute to test whether the
    addresses we have identified as possible victims were indeed generated with
    low entropy", and calls the work its best effort on short notice, which
    "should not be considered to be complete or definitive".
    <cite><a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-methodology-disclaimer/">Galaxy Research, X, 1 Aug 2026</a></cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Galaxy separately reported {fig('attackerHoldingsBtc')} under attacker
    control, with all endpoint attacker addresses fully unspent on chain.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Galaxy's reported total under attacker control and its statement that every endpoint attacker address remains fully unspent"
      source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August thread; the endpoint address set is not published, so the unspent state is not independently checked here"
      href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-attacker-holdings/"
    />
    Those
    endpoints cover all three waves in Galaxy's attributed set. They are not the
    same set as the four consolidation addresses this page checks at block
    960,525, so the two holdings figures are not comparable and neither confirms
    the other. Galaxy does not publish the endpoint addresses, so the unspent
    finding is not rechecked here.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same thread also states that the vulnerable firmware shipped on 17 March
    2021 around block 674,951, and that no coin identified in waves 1 to 3 was
    created before that block.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Galaxy's statement about the firmware ship date, block 674,951 and the creation dates of the coins it identified"
      source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August thread; what the boundary means for published version ranges is set out on the firmware page"
      href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-firmware-block-boundary/"
    />
    That bears on the affected firmware range rather
    than on the accounting here, and is
    <a href="/record/firmware/">taken up on the firmware page</a>.
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

