  <Disclosure id="wave-4" title="A reported fourth wave, corrected twice" note="pattern-matched, no direct victim confirmation reported">

  <p>
    On 2 and 3 August 2026 a fourth-wave claim circulated: pending mempool
    sweeps of transactions that had opted into replace-by-fee, described as a
    coordinated fourth wave. The destination list behind it was circulated by
    the @intangiblecoins account, which then published two corrections to its
    own material. Both corrections are held, and nothing in wave 4 enters the
    accounting elsewhere on this page.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The existence and description of the circulated fourth-wave claim, as restated in the author's own corrections"
      source="The captured @intangiblecoins correction posts of 3 August 2026, which describe and amend the circulated wave-4 material"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-address-correction/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The first correction, posted at 01:37 UTC on 3 August, removed six of 216
    destination addresses because they had years of transaction history before
    block 960,183 on 30 July 2026, which the author judged unlikely for
    attacker addresses. Of the remaining addresses, 206 were stated to have
    been freshly created to receive the sweeps.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The removal of six of 216 wave-4 destination addresses for prior history, and the statement that 206 of the rest were freshly created"
      source="The first captured @intangiblecoins wave-4 correction, 3 Aug 2026"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-address-correction/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The second correction, posted at 03:22 UTC and credited to Nunchuk, states
    that the circulated set erroneously included multisig addresses, while
    waves 1 to 3 contain none. It gives wave 4 as circulated at 857 addresses
    and 486.11 BTC, a multisig discount of 89 addresses and 20.58 BTC, and a
    surviving core of 709 addresses and 448.73 BTC. The author stresses that
    wave 4 rests on pattern matching, that no direct victim has confirmed a
    wave-4 loss yet, and that the word "likely" is used out of caution.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The second correction's multisig finding, its stated circulated, discounted and surviving wave-4 figures, and its statement that wave 4 rests on pattern matching without direct victim confirmation"
      source="The second captured @intangiblecoins wave-4 correction, 3 Aug 2026, crediting Nunchuk"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The stated figures do not reconcile from the post alone: 857 addresses
    minus the 89-address multisig discount gives 768, not the stated 709, and
    486.11 BTC minus 20.58 BTC gives 465.53 BTC, not the stated 448.73 BTC.
    <Claim
      basis="derived"
      scope="The observation that the second correction's stated subtractions give 768 addresses and 465.53 BTC rather than its stated 709 addresses and 448.73 BTC"
      source="Integer subtraction of the counts and amounts stated in the second correction itself; no other input is used"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/"
    />
    The post does not explain the difference. As with other conflicting
    figures on this page, the numbers are preserved as the source states them
    rather than silently adjusted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Galaxy's revised total spans three waves, so the circulated fourth wave
    sits outside it as well as outside the transaction sets this page
    reconciles. Whether the wave-4 set belongs to the same incident is
    recorded as an open question <a href="#not-yet-known">below</a>.
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

