    characterises the set attributed by Block and Galaxy; it is not a necessary
    property of exploiting the firmware flaw.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reported-handling">Reported handling, and what is not reported</h2>

  <p>
    Two threads about how the incident is being handled appear in the captured
    record and belong beside the accounting. Block said it was sharing the
    relevant information with the appropriate authorities, and that it would
    provide further updates when doing so would not interfere with the
    investigation. Coinkite, through NVK, said it would provide a written
    incident summary specific to a user's loss along with any transaction data
    it could share, for people pursuing a police report, an insurance claim or
    their own investigation, and said it was cooperating with the on-chain
    investigators and with any law-enforcement agency that opens a case.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Block's statement that it is sharing information with authorities and Coinkite's offer of written incident summaries and transaction data"
      source="Clay Garrett's captured three-post attribution thread and NVK's captured apology post, both 31 Jul 2026; neither statement's follow-through is observable from here"
      href="/record/sources/clay-attribution/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    One reported item describes an approach to the operator rather than
    movement of the funds. Bitcoin News reported a dust transaction sent to
    the attacker's largest consolidation address, the 562 BTC holding in the
    table above, carrying an OP_RETURN message that advertised a laundering
    service for a 10 percent cut. The report withholds the embedded Telegram
    handle, says the message's authenticity is unproven, and notes that the
    stolen coins had not moved: someone was approaching the attacker on
    chain, and the transaction is not attacker activity.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The reported OP_RETURN laundering solicitation sent to the largest consolidation address, and the report's own caveats about the withheld handle and unproven authenticity"
      source="Bitcoin News's captured post of 2 Aug 2026; the sender is unidentified and the solicitation's authenticity is unestablished"
      href="/record/sources/bitcoinnews-opreturn-laundering-pitch/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    One custodial response downstream of the cluster is also recorded. After
    <a href="/record/sources/thorn-thorchain-peel/">a captured report</a> that
    part of one victim's roughly 30 BTC was peeled over THORChain and reached
    a gambling site, the site, Duel, replied that it does not freeze balances
    on third-party claims as policy, but offered a one-time exception if the
    victim signs a message from a drained address, signs an affidavit and
    shows Duel the evidence trail. No outcome of that offer appears in
    captures held to 3 August 2026.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Duel's stated no-freeze policy and the conditions of its one-time exception for the reported THORChain-peeled victim funds"
      source="Duel's captured reply of 2 Aug 2026 to the peel report; whether the conditions were met or any balance was frozen is not observable from here"
      href="/record/sources/korraflow-duel-freeze-exception/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    Two further points sit beside that: an absence of any recorded custodial
    action against the four addresses, and the collection route left open by
    <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-methodology-disclaimer/">Galaxy's own methodology disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <Disclosure
    id="handling-absences"
    title="Custodial action, and where Galaxy's set stays open"
    note="two absences in the held record"
  >

  <p>
    Nothing in the held captures records an exchange freeze, a flagging
    commitment or any other custodial action against the four consolidation
    addresses as of 3 August 2026. No captured source names an exchange or
    service that has undertaken to stop or mark deposits from them. That is an
    absence in what has been published and collected here, not a finding that no
    such step has been taken.
    <Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="Whether any exchange or custodial service has frozen or committed to flagging deposits from the four consolidation addresses"
      source="No captured source held by this project reports such a step as of 3 August 2026; the archive cannot observe unpublished compliance action"
      href="/record/changes/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    One collection route is open on the analysis side.
    <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-methodology-disclaimer/">Galaxy's own disclaimer</a>
    records that some of the addresses behind its on-chain topographies were
    received from victim reports on X, and that its analysis "should not be
    considered to be complete or definitive". Its identified set is therefore
    open to further reported attacker and victim addresses rather than closed,
    which is one reason its published total has already moved once.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Galaxy's statement that some initial addresses came from victim reports on X and that its analysis is not complete or definitive"
      source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August methodology disclaimer; whether any address was acted on is not observable from here"
      href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-methodology-disclaimer/"
    />
    This archive
    does not collect or publish victim addresses, and nothing here should be
    read as advice to send an address to a third party.
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure id="causation" title="Causation remains unverified">

  <p>
    The timing and transaction pattern are consistent with the public incident,
    but the blockchain does not identify the hardware or software that generated
    a private key. TFTC explicitly separates its reproduced chain event from the
    unproven claim that every transaction resulted from this firmware flaw.
    <Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="The share of the attributed transaction sets actually caused by the firmware flaw, and the site's use of the word theft, which records the public incident attribution rather than an established finding about authorisation: the transactions, amounts, timing and consolidation reproduce from the pinned dataset, but the chain does not show whether each transfer was authorised or who operated the sweep"
      source="The exact share caused by this firmware flaw has not been established from public evidence"
      href="/record/sources/tftc-who-must-move/"
    />
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure id="not-yet-known" title="Not yet known">

  <ul class="node-list">
    <li>
      <strong>How many people were hit.</strong> Source-address counts and
      people are not equivalent. One person or entity can control multiple
      addresses, and an address can have multiple controllers. The number of
      distinct affected owners is unknown.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whether the 695 earlier transactions are the same operator.</strong>
      Block's fingerprint match is reported but not independently confirmed.
      These transactions occur in blocks 960,183 and 960,185, minutes before the
      500-transaction set and within Galaxy's published window. They are not
      evidence by themselves of activity on an earlier date.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="Whether the earlier 695 transactions were controlled by the same operator"
        source="Block's captured preliminary accounting thread, which explicitly says the relationship to the drain was not confirmed"
        href="/record/sources/clay-earlier-waves-thread/"
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whether the reported 31 July cluster is related.</strong> Kevin
      Kelbie reported 1,216 transactions moving 45.9 BTC after Block's scan end.
      Seven of eight stated markers match, while the replace-by-fee behaviour
      differs. It is tracked separately and is not included in this page's
      principal total.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="Whether the reported 45.9 BTC cluster is related to the principal incident set"
        source="Kevin Kelbie primary post, captured 1 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/kevinkelbie-later-wave/"
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is in Galaxy's third wave.</strong> Galaxy's revised total
      spans three waves and {fig('galaxyRevisedAddresses')}. The third wave is
      not in the pinned dataset behind this page and has not been checked
      transaction by transaction here, so the difference between the two totals
      is not itemised. Galaxy states it has not tested whether the addresses it
      identifies were generated with low entropy.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The composition of Galaxy's third wave and the addresses behind its revised total"
        source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August thread, which publishes totals rather than the underlying transaction or address list"
        href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-third-wave-revision/"
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whether the circulated fourth wave is part of the same incident.</strong>
      The corrected wave-4 set rests on pattern matching, and its own author
      reports no direct victim confirmation as of 3 August 2026. Its stated
      figures, corrections and unreconciled arithmetic are recorded
      <a href="#wave-4">above</a>; none of them enters a total on this page.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="Whether the circulated wave-4 set belongs to the same incident as the attributed transaction sets"
        source="The author's own second correction, which states that wave 4 rests on pattern matching with no direct victim confirmation yet"
        href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/"
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whether the consolidated funds will move, and where.</strong> No
      forward statement is possible. The finding above is fixed to its stated
      06:48 UTC check time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Any recovery, seizure or insurance payout.</strong> None has been
      reported in any captured source as of 2 August 2026.
    </li>
  </ul>

  </Disclosure>

